00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  violinist charles yang  and pianist peter dugan have played both  carnegie hall and the kennedy center  [Music]  but these days if you're lucky you'll  find them playing an empty storefront  on a random street corner on manhattan's  upper west side  the musicians aren't exactly roughing it  steinway donated the grand piano  and they're getting a decent salary  thanks to a local philanthropist who  came up with the idea  this is not our typical performance  experience by a long shot but  it's one of the first shows that we've  been able to do  for an actual live audience in  so long we're very grateful that we can  do it here for people that might not  go to a concert hall or an arena and  just hear different kinds of music that  they might not  have liked before  still these are precarious times for  performers  according to the local chapter of the  american federation of musicians  95 percent of their 7 000 local members  are out of work we've never experienced  anything like this  certainly artists careers have pretty  much disappeared  except for online performances and  things like that but it's just not the  same feeling  and of course there's not as much work  to go around concert halls and  performance spaces like the iconic  lincoln center  were among the first places to close in  the pandemic  and they're likely to be the last ones  to reopen not only depriving musicians  of their livelihoods  but also new york of its lifeblood  60 shows with 100 performers have been  scheduled  organizers can't say where or when for  fear of drawing too large a crowd  [Music]  but word is starting to get around this  neighborhood known for the arts  it just gives me this energy and it  makes new york feel a little bit more  the way i think new york should feel to  be that close and be able to see them  the performers interact with each other  was really fascinating i usually can't  afford seats that close  a bit like a musical scavenger hunt in a  time  and a neighborhood where such a  performance is the ultimate prize  kristin salumi al jazeera new york

